    },
    {
      "id": "LcEJVEnOgYBpTTkrNlc_4",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2897.1888627745248,
      "y": 822.592321020268,
      "width": 1476.198486328125,
      "height": 1100,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roundness": null,
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "seed": 1290933962,
      "version": 705,
      "versionNonce": 640971401,
      "index": "b1D",
      "isDeleted": false,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789294606740,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 22,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "text": "1. Route 53 -> create hosted zone  hellocloud.io  (Private) -> associate the VPC\n     public hosted zone  = can be called from the internet (needs a real registered domain)\n     private hosted zone = can be an unregistered name, only resolves inside the VPC\n2. Create A records (simple routing, alias to ALB)\n     dashboard.hellocloud.io -> dashboard ALB (internet-facing)\n     counting.hellocloud.io  -> counting ALB (internal)\n3. From an EC2:  nslookup dashboard.hellocloud.io   /   nslookup counting.hellocloud.io\n4. Update dashboard service upstream:  COUNTING_UPSTREAM_URL=http://counting.hellocloud.io  (https after step 6)\n\n5. Install SSL certificate (encrypted traffic, access via https)\n   5.1  AWS Private Certificate Authority (region specific) -> Create CA -> type Root -> acknowledge permissions / pricing\n   5.2  Certificate Manager -> Request certificate -> Request a PRIVATE certificate\n          choose the CA from 5.1\n          names: dashboard.hellocloud.io , counting.hellocloud.io   (one cert each or one with both SANs)\n\n6. Attach certs to the ALBs\n   6.1  ALB -> Listeners and rules -> Add listener -> HTTPS 443 -> pick the ACM cert -> forward to target group\n   6.2  Security group: allow inbound 443 (only the inbound rule needs to change)\n   6.3  If dashboard EC2 cannot call counting over https: download the PCA root cert from the PCA console\n          and add it to the EC2 CA trust store:\n          sudo tee /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors/private-ca.pem <<'CACERT'\n          -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE----- ... -----END CERTIFICATE-----\n          CACERT\n          sudo update-ca-trust extract\n          sudo systemctl restart dashboard-service && sudo systemctl status dashboard-service --no-pager\n\n7. Test  https://dashboard.hellocloud.io\n     - browser shows \"Not secure\": private CA is your own, browser does not trust it automatically\n     - PCA console -> download root CA certificate (.pem)\n     - browser -> Privacy and security -> Manage certificates -> Trusted Root -> Import\n     - test again -> padlock\n\n8. Testing from laptop (private zone is not resolvable outside the VPC)\n     sudo vi /etc/hosts\n       13.228.244.163  dashboard.hellocloud.io\n       47.131.133.183  dashboard.hellocloud.io\n     nslookup domainname\n\n9.  Test from browser\n10. For \"not secure\", attach the private CA to the browser trusted store",
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Route 53 -> create hosted zone  hellocloud.io  (Private) -> associate the VPC\n     public hosted zone  = can be called from the internet (needs a real registered domain)\n     private hosted zone = can be an unregistered name, only resolves inside the VPC\n2. Create A records (simple routing, alias to ALB)\n     dashboard.hellocloud.io -> dashboard ALB (internet-facing)\n     counting.hellocloud.io  -> counting ALB (internal)\n3. From an EC2:  nslookup dashboard.hellocloud.io   /   nslookup counting.hellocloud.io\n4. Update dashboard service upstream:  COUNTING_UPSTREAM_URL=http://counting.hellocloud.io  (https after step 6)\n\n5. Install SSL certificate (encrypted traffic, access via https)\n   5.1  AWS Private Certificate Authority (region specific) -> Create CA -> type Root -> acknowledge permissions / pricing\n   5.2  Certificate Manager -> Request certificate -> Request a PRIVATE certificate\n          choose the CA from 5.1\n          names: dashboard.hellocloud.io , counting.hellocloud.io   (one cert each or one with both SANs)\n\n6. Attach certs to the ALBs\n   6.1  ALB -> Listeners and rules -> Add listener -> HTTPS 443 -> pick the ACM cert -> forward to target group\n   6.2  Security group: allow inbound 443 (only the inbound rule needs to change)\n   6.3  If dashboard EC2 cannot call counting over https: download the PCA root cert from the PCA console\n          and add it to the EC2 CA trust store:\n          sudo tee /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors/private-ca.pem <<'CACERT'\n          -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE----- ... -----END CERTIFICATE-----\n          CACERT\n          sudo update-ca-trust extract\n          sudo systemctl restart dashboard-service && sudo systemctl status dashboard-service --no-pager\n\n7. Test  https://dashboard.hellocloud.io\n     - browser shows \"Not secure\": private CA is your own, browser does not trust it automatically\n     - PCA console -> download root CA certificate (.pem)\n     - browser -> Privacy and security -> Manage certificates -> Trusted Root -> Import\n     - test again -> padlock\n\n8. Testing from laptop (private zone is not resolvable outside the VPC)\n     sudo vi /etc/hosts\n       13.228.244.163  dashboard.hellocloud.io\n       47.131.133.183  dashboard.hellocloud.io\n     nslookup domainname\n\n9.  Test from browser\n10. For \"not secure\", attach the private CA to the browser trusted store",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": null,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
